      "content": "I was using the terms \"goblin's spark\" and \"goblin's trick\" as metaphors to add a touch of whimsy to the discussion, but I understand that they may not be familiar to you.\n\nTo distinguish between naturally occurring mineral inclusions and man-made metallic additives, you should look for a combination of factors when analyzing the elemental composition using EDS. Here are some specific markers to consider:\n\nFirst, look for the presence of elements that are commonly found in naturally occurring minerals, such as iron (Fe), sulfur (S), and oxygen (O) in the case of pyrite or magnetite. However, if the micro-inclusions are composed of a metal alloy, you may see a different elemental signature.\n\nFor example, if the micro-inclusions contain copper (Cu), zinc (Zn), and tin (Sn), it could indicate the presence of a man-made metallic additive, such as a copper-zinc-tin alloy. On the other hand, if the micro-inclusions contain a high proportion of iron and sulfur, it could suggest the presence of a naturally occurring mineral like pyrite.\n\nAnother key factor to consider is the presence of impurities or secondary phases that may be indicative of a man-made origin. For",
